use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Dragging import Dragging
from ../../../behaviors/FormParticipation import FormParticipation
from ../../../behaviors/Presence import Presence
from ../../../behaviors/Traversal import Traversal
from ../../fields/TextField import TextField
from ../../layout/Grid import Grid
from ../../overlays/Popover import Popover
from ../../ranges/Slider import Slider

export ui-component ColorPicker:
    description:
        A color picker holds a colour as its value. The user can pick it from a
        spread of colours, choose from a set of ready-made swatches, or type a
        colour code. It can also include how see-through the colour is.

    composes:
        - @{Popover}
        - @{Slider}
        - @{TextField}
        - @{Grid}

    behaviors:
        - @{Traversal}
        - @{Dragging}
        - @{FormParticipation}
        - @{Presence}

    trigger:
        A small button showing the current colour opens the picker in a popup.
        The component can instead show the picker in place, always open, with
        no button or popup.

    parts:
        The component chooses which of these to show: a colour area with a
        hue slider, a set of swatches, a box for typing a colour code, boxes
        for each part of the colour such as red, green and blue, and a preview
        of the chosen colour. Changing any one updates the others.

    colourArea:
        The user drags a point around a square of colours, or clicks to move it
        there, or moves it with the arrow keys. A slider beside it picks the
        hue. The component is told about changes continuously while dragging,
        and again once when the user lets go.

    swatches:
        Swatches are laid out in a grid. Each has a name that assistive tools
        read out, such as "Ocean blue", rather than a code. The user moves
        between them with the arrow keys. The component decides whether
        picking a swatch closes the popup.

    typing:
        The user can type a colour code. It's read when they finish, and if it
        isn't a valid colour, an error is shown and the value doesn't change.
        Which formats are accepted, such as hex codes, is up to the chosen
        library.

    transparency:
        If the component allows it, a second slider sets how see-through the
        colour is.

    disabled:
        A disabled color picker can't be opened or changed. A read-only one
        shows its colour and can be opened and read out, but not changed.

    nonGoals:
        - Picking a colour from elsewhere on screen is up to the chosen library
            and platform
        - Which colour spaces are supported, beyond standard screen colours, is
            up to the chosen library
